  "answer_text": "There are **three ways** to change a `<span>`'s color on mouse over. Here they are, from simplest to most advanced:\n\n---\n\n## \u2705 Method 1: CSS `:hover` Pseudo-class (Recommended)\n\nThis is the simplest and cleanest approach.\n\n```html\n<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html>\n  <head>\n    <style>\n      span {\n        color: black;\n        cursor: pointer;\n      }\n\n      span:hover {\n        color: red;\n      }\n    </style>\n  </head>\n  <body>\n    <span>Hover over me!</span>\n  </body>\n</html>\n```\n\nIf you can identify an element by its tag, id (`#`), or class (`.`), you can add the `:hover` selector to make the changes only appear when the user has moused over an element.\n\n---\n\n## \u2705 Method 2: Inline JavaScript with `onmouseover` / `onmouseout`\n\nYou can use inline event handlers directly on the `<span>` tag:\n\n```html\n<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html>\n  <body>\n    <span\n      onmouseover=\"this.style.color='red';\"\n      onmouseout=\"this.style.color='black';\"\n    >\n      Hover over me!\n    </span>\n  </body>\n</html>\n```\n\nThe JavaScript code must be attached inside quotes to an HTML tag using event handlers like `onmouseover` and `onmouseout`. Inside those quotes, use the JavaScript keyword `this` to refer automatically to whatever HTML element the user is interacting with.\n\nNote that hyphenated CSS attributes like `background-color` must be converted to camelCase JavaScript ones like `backgroundColor`.\n\n---\n\n## \u2705 Method 3: External JavaScript with `addEventListener`\n\nThis is the most flexible and scalable approach for larger projects:\n\n```html\n<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html>\n  <body>\n    <span id=\"mySpan\">Hover over me!</span>\n\n    <script>\n      const span = document.getElementById(\"mySpan\");\n\n      span.addEventListener(\"mouseover\", function () {\n        this.style.color = \"red\";\n      });\n\n      span.addEventListener(\"mouseout\", function () {\n        this.style.color = \"black\";\n      });\n    </script>\n  </body>\n</html>\n```\n\nThe `addEventListener()` function is employed to associate an event handler with a specific element. The `mouseover` event is used to change the color of an HTML element, and the `mouseout` event is used to restore the color after removing the mouse from the element.\n\n---\n\n## \ud83c\udfc6 Which Method Should You Use?\n\n| Method | Best For |\n|---|---|\n| **CSS `:hover`** | Simple, clean styling \u2014 always preferred |\n| **Inline JS** | Quick testing or very simple one-off cases |\n| **`addEventListener`** | Dynamic/complex behavior in larger projects |\n\nFor most use cases, **Method 1 (CSS `:hover`)** is the recommended approach since it keeps styling concerns in CSS and is the most maintainable.",
